.TH NOHUP "1" "September 2026" "GNU coreutils 9.12" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nohup \- run a command immune to hangups, with output to a non-tty
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nohup
\fI\,COMMAND \/\fR[\fI\,ARG\/\fR]...
.br
.B nohup
\fI\,OPTION\/\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.\" Add any additional description here
.PP
Run COMMAND, ignoring hangup signals.
.TP
\X'tty: link https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/nohup#nohup--help'\fB\-\-help\fP\X'tty: link'
display this help and exit
.TP
\X'tty: link https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/nohup#nohup--version'\fB\-\-version\fP\X'tty: link'
output version information and exit
.PP
If standard input is a terminal, redirect it from an unreadable file.
If standard output is a terminal, append output to 'nohup.out' if possible,
\&'$HOME/nohup.out' otherwise.
If standard error is a terminal, redirect it to standard output.
To save output to FILE, use 'nohup COMMAND > FILE'.
.PP
Your shell may have its own version of nohup, which usually supersedes
the version described here.  Please refer to your shell's documentation
for details about the options it supports.
.SS "Exit status:"
.PP
.PD 0
.TP 5
125
if the nohup command itself fails
.TP 5
126
if COMMAND is found but cannot be invoked
.TP 5
127
if COMMAND cannot be found
.TP 5
\-
the exit status of COMMAND otherwise
.PD
